\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}
\usepackage[margin=25mm]{geometry}
\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsthm,longtable,array}
\setlength{\emergencystretch}{2em}
\newtheorem{lemma}{Lemma}\newtheorem{theorem}[lemma]{Theorem}
\title{Seven pentagons through every edge}
\author{Human Compression project}\date{}
\begin{document}\maketitle
We prove an all-order exclusion by reducing the required local
neighbourhoods to a finite certificate. The reduction and every
rejection criterion are proved below. The certificate contains explicit
short cycles or literal graph isomorphisms; its verifier uses no graph
isomorphism library and no unbounded search.

\begin{theorem}
There is no finite simple quartic graph of girth five with exactly
seven pentagons through each edge. Thus $n(4,5,7)=\infty$.
\end{theorem}

Fix a hypothetical graph and a vertex $v$. Its four neighbours are
$p_0,\ldots,p_3$, and their other neighbours form four disjoint
triples $L_i$. No $L_i$ contains an edge. The induced graph $H$ on
these twelve leaves has at most a matching between any two branches:
two neighbours in one branch would create a quadrangle through its
parent. Write $m_{ij}\le3$ for the matching size.
Pentagons through $vp_i$ correspond bijectively to leaf edges incident
with branch $i$, so
\[
 \sum_{j\ne i}m_{ij}=7,\qquad
 \sum_{j\ne i}(3-m_{ij})=2.                         \tag{1}
\]

\begin{lemma}\label{inventory}
Up to relabelling the branches and leaves, $H$ is included in the
following complete inventory.
\begin{enumerate}
\item $m_{01}=m_{23}=1$ and the other four matchings are complete.
Normalize the two single edges to join the first leaves in their
respective branches. There remain $6^4=1296$ choices for the four
complete matchings.
\item $m_{01}=m_{23}=3$ and the other four matchings have size two.
Normalize the two complete matchings to be identities. Each remaining
matching has $3\cdot3\cdot2=18$ choices, giving $18^4=104976$ cases.
\end{enumerate}
Among these $106272$ labelled cases, $105176$ have a triangle or
quadrangle. Each of the other $1096$ is isomorphic, fixing the root
of the ball, to one of the eight representatives in the appendix.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The numbers $3-m_{ij}$ define a loopless multigraph of degree two
on four branch labels. It is either two disjoint double edges or a
four-cycle. These give the two stated cases. The normalizations use
independent relabellings within disjoint pairs of branches, so they
lose no case. A matching of size two is specified by its unmatched
leaf on each side and one of two bijections between the remaining
leaves.

The final finite assertion is certified in
\texttt{root\_certificate.jsonl}. The verifier constructs each of
the stated Cartesian products itself. For every case the certificate
gives either three or four distinct vertices whose consecutive pairs
are edges, or a permutation of all seventeen vertices fixing $v$.
In the latter case every one of the $17^2$ ordered adjacency entries
is compared with the specified representative. Every inventory key
must occur exactly once. Thus the finite assertion requires neither
the correctness of a canonical labelling program nor an isomorphism
test beyond these literal comparisons. The representative counts are
\[
                   16,144,144,144,144,288,72,144.
\]
The generator used graph software to discover the maps; the verifier
only checks their stated permutations.
\end{proof}

Let $B$ be the induced ball of radius two about $v$, including
$H$, the four parents and the root. Each leaf $x$ has unfinished
degree $s_x=4-\deg_B(x)=3-\deg_H(x)$. Equation (1) gives fourteen
leaf edges, so
\[
                         \sum_{x\in H}s_x=36-28=8.       \tag{2}
\]
All unfinished edges go to vertices outside $B$. Let $Z$ consist
of those outside vertices adjacent to at least one leaf. Regard the
eight incidences in (2) as distinct objects, even when two belong to
the same leaf. Their endpoints in $Z$ partition them into nonempty
blocks. Each block has size at most four, contains no repeated leaf,
and contains no two leaves at distance one or two in $B$. These
conditions follow respectively from degree four, simplicity, and the
absence of triangles and quadrangles.

\begin{lemma}\label{boundary}
All possible attachments from $B$ to $Z$ occur in the following finite
enumeration. Order the eight incidences by leaf label. Process them
in that order, placing the next incidence either in any existing block
which obeys the preceding conditions, or in one new last block.
For the eight representatives the enumeration has, respectively,
\[
                     354,436,195,631,253,127,69,141
\]
outputs, totalling $2206$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
In any actual attachment, label the vertices of $Z$ in order of the
first incidence reaching them. The resulting blocks are produced by
the enumeration: their prefixes obey every required condition. If a
leaf has two incidences, give these any fixed order. The procedure
may list isomorphic attachments more than once, which affects neither
coverage nor rejection. The stated finite counts are obtained by
running exactly this recursion in the verifier. No bound on the total
order of the original graph is used.
\end{proof}

For an enumerated attachment, let $B'$ contain $B$, the new vertices
$Z$, and the attachment edges, but initially no edges inside $Z$.
If $B'$ has a triangle or quadrangle it is impossible. Otherwise
write $P_e$ for the number of pentagons in $B'$ through an edge $e$.
These are counted exactly by the simple paths of length four between
the endpoints of $e$. If any $P_e>7$, the attachment is impossible,
since later edges cannot remove a cycle.

Only the twelve edges from a parent to its leaves are needed for the
remaining test. For such an edge $e=p_i x$, and a proposed edge
$f=zz'$ inside $Z$, put
\[
 a_e(f)=
 \boldsymbol1_{x\sim z}\,|N_{B'}(z')\cap(L_i\setminus\{x\})|
 +\boldsymbol1_{x\sim z'}\,|N_{B'}(z)\cap(L_i\setminus\{x\})|.
                                                               \tag{3}
\]
Let $\mathcal F$ consist of all unordered pairs of distinct vertices
of $Z$ whose distance in $B'$ is at least four, and for which
$a_e(f)\le7-P_e$ for every parent--leaf edge $e$.

\begin{lemma}\label{capacity}
For every parent--leaf edge in any completion,
\[
                   7\le P_e+\sum_{f\in\mathcal F}a_e(f).        \tag{4}
\]
Consequently failure of (4) for one edge excludes the attachment
at every total order.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Consider a pentagon through $p_i x$ and follow its remaining four
edges from $x$ back to $p_i$. The last vertex before $p_i$ is the
root or another leaf of $L_i$. In the first case the whole cycle
lies in $B$. In the second, the two intermediate vertices either
both lie in $B$, exactly one lies in $Z$, or both lie in $Z$.
The first two possibilities are already counted in $P_e$. The
last uses exactly one edge $zz'$ inside $Z$, and (3) counts its
possible final leaves. No vertex outside $B\cup Z$ can occur in
such a cycle, since both intermediate vertices are adjacent to a
leaf. No such pentagon uses two edges inside $Z$.

Every actual edge inside $Z$ has distance at least four between
its endpoints in $B'$, or it would close a triangle or quadrangle.
Its contribution to any one edge cannot exceed that edge's residual
allowance $7-P_e$. It therefore belongs to $\mathcal F$. The
actual number of pentagons through $e$ is $P_e$ plus the sum of
(3) over the edges inside $Z$ which are present. Summing instead
over all of $\mathcal F$ can only increase this number, proving
(4). It is harmless that the pairs in $\mathcal F$ might not be
simultaneously realizable or might violate other degree bounds.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Proof of the theorem]
Apply Lemmas~\ref{inventory} and \ref{boundary} to the hypothetical
root ball and its actual outside neighbours. Every enumerated
attachment is excluded by one of the three necessary tests just
proved. The certificate gives the following complete accounting:
\[
\begin{array}{c|r|rrr}
 \text{representative}&\text{attachments}&
 \text{short cycle}& P_e>7 &\text{failure of (4)}\\\hline
 0&354&0&246&108\\
 1&436&40&310&86\\
 2&195&0&65&130\\
 3&631&0&407&224\\
 4&253&0&182&71\\
 5&127&0&5&122\\
 6&69&0&0&69\\
 7&141&0&0&141
\end{array}
\]
For a short cycle the certificate supplies its vertices. For excess
pentagons it supplies eight distinct simple paths of length four
between the indicated edge's endpoints. For a failure of (4), the
verifier recounts all such paths in $B'$, enumerates every pair in
$Z$, computes (3), and checks the strict inequality for the indicated
parent--leaf edge. The block sequence and its index are compared
against the complete recursion of Lemma~\ref{boundary}, with neither
missing nor extra records allowed. Thus every actual attachment is
impossible, independently of any vertices farther from the root.
This proves the asserted nonexistence.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Attribution and scope.}
Goedgebeur and Jooken, \emph{Exhaustive generation of edge-girth-regular
graphs}, arXiv:2401.08271v2, Table 2 on physical page 18, record
$n(4,5,7)\ge35$ and no finite upper bound. This theorem implies
that bound and strengthens it to a global exclusion. It does not
refute the source statement. The root-branch and matching-deficit
argument is shared with the preceding proof for eight pentagons.
The present additional work is the complete local inventory and the
parent--leaf capacity certificate. Its finite casework, software
verification and written reduction are explicit proof dependencies.
We make no priority or named-conjecture claim, and assign no
whole-original, Lean or numerical-compression credit to this component.

\clearpage
